  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: This afternoon marks the beginning of a period of \nquiet weather as a drier post-frontal air mass moves in and high \npressure builds.  Any residual showers this afternoon will depart to \nthe east by this evening, and the remainder of the day will be dry. \nWest/northwest winds will continue to gust in the 15 to 30 mph range \nthis afternoon before weakening after sunrise.  Temperatures over \nthe next few days will undergo a gradual warming trend.  Highs \nThursday and Friday will be in the 70s to low 80s, with highs \nwarming to the low to mid 80s areawide by Saturday.  Dewpoints will \nbe noticeably lower for the second half of the week compared to the \nlast few days, making for a comfortable end of the week.  No \nprecipitation is expected through Saturday. KEY MESSAGE 2: High \npressure starts to shift to the east by the second half of the \nweekend, leading to increasing S/SW flow. The result will be warming \ntemperatures, with highs to be mainly in the mid/upper 80s both \nSaturday and Sunday. Humidity doesn't look too bad  as dewpoints are \nexpected to remain the upper 50s to mid 60s. Our next chance of \nprecipitation arrives late Sunday into Monday and likely persists \ninto mid week as upper troughiness sets up across eastern Canada and \nthe northeastern CONUS. Shortwaves rotating around the upper trough \nwill bring periods of showers and thunderstorms. These sorts of \nfeatures are typically difficult to nail down in timing and strength \nthis far out, and indeed, model solutions differ quite a bit from \nSunday night onward. Have stayed with the NBM/WPC forecast for now \ngiven the uncertainty, which results in chances of \nshowers/thunderstorms each day. Temperatures look to remain \nseasonable through the period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure will begin to drift east this weekend \nwith more southerly to southwesterly flow, helping to advect warmer \nair into the region. Temperatures will warm into the low to mid 80s \nin general, with perhaps a few spot upper 80s in the Champlain \nValley. Given an unfavorable moisture advection pattern, dewpoints \nshould remain on the drier side for the weekend, remaining in the \n50s. Warm air advection will keep overnight temperatures on the mild \nside in the low to mid 60s. Subsidence under the high should keep \nany precipitation at bay through most of the weekend with mainly \nclear skies. Our next system is expected to swing through the area \nlate Sunday into early next week. A cyclonic gyre over northern \nQuebec will bring troughiness to the East Coast, with a few \nshortwaves riding along the southern periphery of the gyre through \nthe Great Lakes into our region. There is still quite the spread in \nthe timing and phasing of any of these shortwaves, but trends have \ndenoted best chances for precipitation to be Monday and Tuesday \nafternoons, which is in line with the current WPC forecast. As these \nwaves pass through they will take with them small chunks of heat and \nmoisture, gradually knocking our temperatures and humidity levels \ndown into mid next week.",
